In this lesson we'll look at how to read a data display and, just as importantly, how to explain why you picked it. Picking a chart is really about answering two questions: what does the display actually show, and why is it the right one for this data and this question. A handy sentence to remember is, the data is this type, the question is asking this, so a display like this works because of this reason. When you read any display, start by saying what the axes mean and what units they are in. Then point out the centre, the spread, and the overall shape. Call out any outliers and say why they matter rather than just ignoring them. Finally, tie the pattern back to the real-world context, so you say something like, the data was about this, so the peak at that value tells us this. Now let's see it in action.